        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c658d4e7-3c93-58a7-9378-a70f50be6f55",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John McPhun, Jairos Jiri\n- Places: Rhodesia, Salisbury, Harare, Southern Rhodesia, Zimbabwe\n- Roles: first-class cricketer\n- Organizations/Events: Currie Cup\n- Concepts: responsible government, disability care, Christian charity\n\nFacts:\n- In 1923, Southern Rhodesia achieved responsible government under British colonial rule.\n- Rhodesia, with its capital at Salisbury, was considered the de facto successor state to Southern Rhodesia; Salisbury is now called Harare.\n- John David McPhun was born on September 8, 1940, in Salisbury, Rhodesia.\n- In the 1940s, Jairos Jiri began creating facilities in Rhodesia/Zimbabwe for disadvantaged and disabled people based on Christian principles of charity, patience, and non-judgmental tolerance.\n- John McPhun was a first-class cricketer who played for Rhodesia in the Currie Cup.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "094e4db3-5315-512b-b5d5-33b463ec712e",
        "text": "Page: John McPhun. Sentence: John David McPhun (born September 8, 1940 in Salisbury, Rhodesia) was a first-class cricketer who played for Rhodesia in the Currie Cup.\nPage: Rhodesia. Sentence: With its capital in Salisbury (now Harare), Rhodesia was considered a \"de facto\" successor state to the former British colony of Southern Rhodesia (which had achieved responsible government in 1923).\nPage: Jairos Jiri. Sentence: He started creating facilities in the 1940s for disadvantaged and disabled people in Rhodesia/Zimbabwe, using Christian principles of charity, patience and non-judgmental tolerance.",
